<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/music-history-eras">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Music history is organised into eras — <strong>Baroque, Classical, Romantic, Modern</strong> —
    each with characteristic instruments, forms and social settings. The eras are real (you can
    hear the difference) but also constructions: they describe European art music, while the
    fuller story includes every tradition playing in parallel. Read the eras as chapters, not the
    whole book.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four eras and the social forces inside them" widget="equationStep" config={erasCfg} />

  <h2>Technology and society drive the eras</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Instruments shifted the sound:</strong> valve horns and pianofortes enabled Romantic expressiveness; the electric guitar, the synthesiser and the drum machine created genres, not just sounds.</li>
    <li><strong>Recording (1877→) transformed everything:</strong> music became repeatable, portable and global; performances frozen for comparison; local styles cross-pollinated within a decade. Every era after 1900 is partly a recording-era story.</li>
    <li><strong>Patronage shaped the product:</strong> church → court → public concert → record label → streaming. Who pays shapes what gets written — the same lever the Renaissance lesson traced in painting.</li>
    <li><strong>The canon is a conversation:</strong> Bach was considered old-fashioned in his lifetime, revived by Mendelssohn in 1829; Clara Schumann was a celebrated composer-pianist written out of the canon and written back in. Eras are edited continually.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Grout & Palisca, A History of Western Music; Taruskin, The Oxford History of Western Music; recordings as primary sources.</p>
</TopicPage>
